"If the dose of a drug is 17.5 mg/m²/day, how many milligrams of the drug should be administered daily to a patient weighing 65 lb and measuring 3 ft 6 in. in height?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
If the dose of a drug is 17.5 mg/m²/day, how many milligrams of the drug should be administered daily to a patient weighing 65 lb and measuring 3 ft 6 in. in height?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the drug dose, we first need to find the patient's surface area (in m²) using the Dutard formula:

1. Convert the weight from pounds to kilograms:
   \[
   65 \text{ lb} \times \frac{1 \text{ kg}}{2.2 \text{ lb}} = 32.2 \text{ kg}
   \]

2. Convert the height from feet and inches to meters:
   \[
   3 \text{ ft} \times 30 \text{ cm/ft} + 6 \text{ in} \times 2.5 \text{ cm/in} = 90 \text{ cm} + 15 \text{ cm} = 105 \text{ cm} = 1.05 \text{ m}
   \]

3. Calculate the surface area (A) using the Dutart formula:
   \[
   A = 0.0014 \times \text{Mass}^{0.5} \times \text{Height}^{0.5}
   \]
   \[
   A = 0.0014 \times (32.2)^0.5 \times (1.05)^0.5 \approx 0.014 \text{ m}^2
   \]

4. Multiply the surface area by the drug dose rate:
   \[
   \text{Dose} = 17.5 \text{ mg/m}^2 \times 0.014 \text{ m}^2 \approx 0.24 \text{ mg}
   \]

Thus, the drug dose for this person is approximately \(\boxed{0.24 \text{ mg}}\).
