A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The maximum height \( h \) is reached when the particle's velocity is zero.  
At the maximum height, the particle's velocity \( v \) is \( 0 \, \text{m/s} \).  
The initial velocity \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \), and the acceleration \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \).  

Using the kinematic equation:  
\[ v^2 = u^2 + 2 a h \]  
Substituting the known values:  
\[ 0^2 = (20)^2 + 2 (-9.8) h \]  
\[ 0 = 400 - 19.6 h \]  
\[ 19.6 h = 400 \]  
\[ h = \frac{400}{19.6} \]  
\[ h = 20.408 \, \text{m} \]  

Thus, the maximum height reached by the particle is \(\boxed{20.408 \, \text{m}}\).

