","The solubility of sulfides varies significantly. To determine which sulfides precipitate, we compare the solubility product constants (\( K_{sp} \)) of the sulfides formed with \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \). The sulfides with \( K_{sp} \) values less than \( K_{sp} \) of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) (which is \( 10^{-22} \)) will precipitate. 

The solubility products of the sulfides are as follows:
- \( \text{MnS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-19} \)
- \( \text{NiS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-19} \)
- \( \text{CuS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-29} \)
- \( \text{HgS} \): \( K_{sp} = 10^{-49} \)

Comparing these values to \( K_{sp} \) of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \):
- \( \text{MnS} \), \( \text{NiS} \), and \( \text{CuS} \) have \( K_{sp} \) values less than \( K_{sp} \) of \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \), so they will precipitate.
- \( \text{HgS} \) has a much lower \( K_{sp} \), so it is even less soluble and will precipitate.

Thus, the sulfides that precipitate are:
- \( \text{MnS} \)
- \( \text{NiS} \)
- \( \text{CuS} \)
- \( \text{HgS} \)

Thus, all the sulfides of the given metal ions will precipitate.
