","The reaction between solid benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is a neutralization reaction. The observable effects of this reaction are:  
1. The solid benzoic acid dissolves in the solution.  
2. The solution becomes cold due to the exothermic nature of the reaction.  
3. A white precipitate of sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) forms.  

The fully ionized equation for the reaction is:  
\[ \text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(s)} + \text{NaOH(aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(aq)} + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]  

**Explanation:**  
- Benzoic acid (H(C₇H₅O₂)) is a weak acid, and sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is a strong base.  
- When they react, the acid donates a proton (H⁺) to the base, forming sodium benzoate (Na(C₇H₅O₂)) and water (H₂O).  
- The reaction is exothermic, meaning it releases heat.  
- The sodium benzoate is a salt and does not dissolve in water, forming a white precipitate.  

Thus, the balanced equation shows the complete ionization of the reactants and products.  

\[ \boxed{\text{H(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(s)} + \text{NaOH(aq)} \rightarrow \text{Na(C}_7\text{H}_5\text{O}_2(aq)} + \text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]
