A solution containing 0.5126 g of naphthalene (molecular weight = 128.17 g/mol) in 50 g of carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄) exhibits a boiling point elevation of 0.402°C. Another solution containing 0.6216 g of an unknown solute in the same mass of CCl₄ shows a boiling point elevation of 0.647°C. Calculate the molecular weight of the unknown solute.,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
A solution containing 0.5126 g of naphthalene (molecular weight = 128.17 g/mol) in 50 g of carbon tetrachloride (CCl₄) exhibits a boiling point elevation of 0.402°C. Another solution containing 0.6216 g of an unknown solute in the same mass of CCl₄ shows a boiling point elevation of 0.647°C. Calculate the molecular weight of the unknown solute.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the molecular weight of the unknown solute, we use the formula for boiling point elevation:

ΔT = K * m

where ΔT is the boiling point elevation, K is the ebullioscopic constant of the solvent (CCl₄), and m is the molality of the solution.

First, we need to find the molality of the naphthalene solution:

m_naphthalene = moles of naphthalene / mass of solvent (in kg)

moles of naphthalene = mass of naphthalene / molecular weight of naphthalene
moles of naphthalene = 0.5126 g / 128.17 g/mol = 0.00389 mol

m_naphthalene = 0.00389 mol / 0.050 kg = 0.0778 mol/kg

Now, we can find the ebullioscopic constant (K) of CCl₄:

K = ΔT / m_naphthalene
K = 0.402°C / 0.0778 mol/kg = 5.69 K/mol%

Now, we can find the molality of the unknown solute solution:

m_unknown = ΔT / K
m_unknown = 0.647°C / 5.69 K/mol% = 0.109 mol/kg

Now, we can find the molecular weight of the unknown solute:

moles of unknown solute = m_unknown * mass of solvent (in kg)
moles of unknown solute = 0.109 mol/kg * 0.050 kg = 0.00545 mol

Molecular weight of unknown solute = mass of unknown solute / moles of unknown solute
Molecular weight of unknown solute = 0.6216 g / 0.00545 mol = 113.5 g/mol

So, the molecular weight of the unknown solute is 113.5 g/mol.
