In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ (sigma) and π (pi) molecular orbitals lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbitals**: The electron density is concentrated along the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This results in a symmetrical distribution of electron density.  
- **π molecular orbitals**: The electron density is concentrated above and below the axis between the nuclei of the bonded atoms. This results in a non-symmetrical distribution of electron density.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by the overlap of atomic orbitals along the axis, while π orbitals are formed by the overlap of atomic orbitals parallel to the axis.  

$\boxed{\text{σ: Electron density along the axis, symmetrical}}$  
$\boxed{\text{π: Electron density above/below the axis, non-symmetrical}}$
