","To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (ΔG°) for the electrochemical reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔG° = -nFE°

where n is the number of moles of electrons transferred, F is the Faraday constant (96,485 C/mol), and E° is the standard cell potential.

First, we need to determine the standard cell potential (E°) for the reaction. We can do this by subtracting the standard reduction potential of the less-powerful half-cell from the standard reduction potential of the more-powerful half-cell. In this case, the Cu2+/Cu half-cell is more-powerful (higher E°) than the Zn2+/Zn half-cell. Therefore:

E° = E°(Cu2+|Cu) - E°(Zn2+|Zn)
E° = (+0.34 V) - (-0.76 V)
E° = +1.10 V

Now, we need to determine the number of moles of electrons (n) transferred in the reaction. In this case, one mole of electrons is transferred from Zn to Cu2+ to form Zn2+ and Cu. So, n = 1.

Now we can calculate the Gibbs free energy change:

ΔG° = -nFE°
ΔG° = -(1 mol)(96,485 C/mol)(1.10 V)
ΔG° = -116,933.5 J/mol

Since the standard cell potential is positive, the reaction is spontaneous and the Gibbs free energy change is negative, indicating that the reaction will proceed spontaneously under standard conditions.
