","To determine the change in equilibrium concentration of FeCN$_6^{4-}$, we first need to write the balanced chemical equation for the formation of the complex ion:

Fe$^{3+} + 6CN^- \rightleftharpoons FeCN_6^{4-}$

Next, we need to find the initial concentrations of the reactants:

[Fe$^{3+}] = 1.0 \, \text{M}$ (since the initial solution is 1.0 M FeCl$_3$)
[CN$^-] = 0.25 \, \text{M}$ (since 0.25 moles of KCN are added to 1 L of solution)

Now, we can use the K$_{eq}$ value to determine the equilibrium concentrations of the species involved in the reaction. Let's set up an ICE (Initial, Change, Equilibrium) table:

| Species        | Initial (M) | Change (M) | Equilibrium (M) |
|----------------|----------|-----|-------------------|
| Fe$^{3+}$        | 1.0      | -x      | 1.0 - x        |
| CN$^-$        | 0.25      | -6x      | 0.25 - 6x        |
| FeCN$_6^{4-}$      | 0      | +x      | x        |

Since the K$_{eq}$ value is very large (1.2 x 10$^{20}$), the reaction will proceed almost to completion, meaning that x will be very close to 0.25 (since 6x will be much smaller than 0.25). Therefore, we can approximate the equilibrium concentrations as:

[Fe$^{3+}] \approx 1.0 - x \approx 1.0 \, \text{M}
[CN$^-] \approx 0.25 - 6x \approx 0.25 \, \text{M}
[FeCN_6^{4-}] \approx x \approx 0.25 \, \text{M}

The change in equilibrium concentration of FeCN$_6^{4-}$ is approximately 0.25 M.
