Write an equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The equation of the tangent line to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by:  
\[ y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a) \]  
This is derived from the point-slope form of the equation of a line:  
\[ y - f(a) = m(x - a) \]  
where \( m \) is the slope of the tangent line. The slope \( m \) is given by the derivative of \( f \) at \( x = a \), i.e., \( f'(a) \).  

Thus, the equation \( y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a) \) represents the tangent line to the graph of \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)] \).
