","To find the Fourier transform of the function \( f(t) = 3 \sin(2\pi t) + 2 \cos(4\pi t) \), we use the definition of the Fourier transform:

\[
f(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) e^{-\omega t} \, dt
\]

Substituting \( f(t) \):

\[
f(\omega) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \left( 3 \sin(2\pi t) + 2 \cos(4\pi t) \right) e^{-\omega t} \, dt
\]

This integral can be split into two separate integrals:

\[
f(\omega) = 3 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \sin(2\pi t) e^{-\omega t} \, dt + 2 \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \cos(4\pi t) e^{-\omega t} \, dt
\]

Each integral can be evaluated using standard integration techniques (e.g., integration by parts). The results are:

\[
f(\infty) = \frac{3 \cdot 2\pi}{(\omega)^2 + (2\pi)^2} + \frac{2 \cdot 4\pi}{(\omega)^2 + (4\pi)^2}
\]

Thus, the Fourier transform of \( f(t) \) is:

\[
f(\omega) = \frac{6\pi}{(\omega)^2 + (2\pi)^2} + \frac{8\pi}{(\omega)^2 + (4\pi)^2}
\]

This result shows that the function \( f(t) \) consists of two harmonic components: one at \( \omega = 2\pi \) and one at \( \omega = 4\pi \). The amplitudes of these components are \( 6\pi \) and \( 8\pi \), respectively.
