","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in QuickScreen, assuming equal-size splits, is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1 \\
C(n/2) + C(n/2) + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1
\end{cases}
\]
where \( C(n) \) is the number of comparisons required to process a list of size \( n \).

**Explanation:**
1. **Case Case \( n = 1 \):**
   - A single element requires no comparisons, so \( C(1) = 0 \).
   - This is the base case of the recurrence.

2. **Case \( n > 1 \):**
   - The number of comparisons is:
     - \( C(n/2) + C(n/2) \): The number of comparisons required to process the two equal-size subleaves (since the pivot splits the input into two equal-size subleaves).
     - \( n - 1 \): The number of comparisons to compare the pivot to all \( n - 1 \) other elements.

Thus, the total number of comparisons is \( C(n/2) + C(n/2) + n - 1 \).

This recurrence relation reflects the division of the input into two equal-size subcases and the additional comparisons required to compare the pivot against the other elements.
