","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton effect formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,  
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.054 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),  
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.11 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),  
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),  
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.  

The maximum change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (i.e., the electron is scattered head-to-back-on), so \(\cos \theta = -1\). Substituting the values:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi (1.054 \times 10^{-34})}{9.11 \times 10^{-31} \times 3 \times 10^8} (1 + 1) = 2.28 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} = 0.228 \, \text{Å}
\]  

The maximum percentage change is:  
\[
\frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_0} \times 100 = \frac{0.228}{2.00} \times 100 = 11.4\%
\]  

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of the photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{11.4\%}\).
