","The Dufort–Frankel method is a second-order parabolic equation in time and space. The stability of the method is determined by the coefficient of \(\phi_{j}^{n+1}\) in the equation. If the coefficient is negative, the solution will be stable, as the equation will damp out oscillations. If the coefficient is positive, the solution will be unstable, as the equation will cause oscillations to grow.

The coefficient of \(\phi_{j}^{n+1}\) in the Dufort–Frankel equation is:
\[
1 - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2}.
\]
To ensure stability, this coefficient must be negative. Solving for \(\Delta t\):
\[
1 - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} < 0 \implies \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} > 1 \implies \Delta t < \frac{(\Delta x)^2}{2\kappa}.
\]
This is the stability condition for the Dufort–Frankel method. If \(\Delta t\) satisfies this condition, the solution will be stable and not grow. 

Thus, the Dufort–Frankel method provides stable, non-growing solutions for any \(\Delta t\) less than \(\frac{(\Delta x)^2}{2\kappa}\). 

\boxed{\text{The Dufort–Frankel method is stable for } \Delta t < \frac{(\Delta x)^2}{2\kappa}}.
