","To estimate the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength, we use the Oleyley-type equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + \frac{G b^2}{a^2}
\]  
where:  
- \(\sigma_{yt}\) is the tensile yield strength of the pure alloy (assumed to be known).  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus.  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector.  
- \(a\) is the center-to-center spacing of the particles.  

Substituting the given values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + \frac{26 \times 10^9 \text{ GN m}^{-2} \times (0.286 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m})^2}{(6 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m})^2}
