A polycrystalline aluminium alloy contains hard particles with a diameter of \(10^{-8}\) m and an average center-to-center spacing of \(6 \times 10^{-8}\) m in the slip planes. The shear modulus \(G\) of aluminium is 26 GN m\(^{-2}\), and the Burgers vector \(b\) is 0.286 nm. Estimate the contribution of these particles to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) of the alloy.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To estimate the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength, we use the Oleyley-type equation:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + \frac{G b^2}{a^2}
\]  
where:  
- \(\sigma_{yt}\) is the tensile yield strength of the pure alloy (assumed to be known).  
- \(G\) is the shear modulus.  
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector.  
- \(a\) is the center-to-center spacing of the particles.  

Substituting the given values:  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + \frac{26 \times 10^9 \text{ GN m}^{-2} \times (0.286 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m})^2}{(6 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m})^2}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + \frac{26 \times 10^9 \times 0.286^2 \times 10^{-18}}{36 \times 10^{-16}}
\]  
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yt} + 2.2 \text{ GN m}^{-2}
\]  

Thus, the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{2.2 \text{ GN m}^{-2}}\).

</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

