The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) in a Cartesian coordinate system is given by the following expression:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_x & V_y & V_z \end{vmatrix}
\]
where \(\mathbf{i}\), \(\mathbf{j}\), and \(\mathbf{k}\) are the unit vectors along the \(x\), \(y\), and \(z\) axes, respectively, and \(V_x\), \(V_y\), and \(V_z\) are the components of \(\mathbf{V}\).

Since \(\mathbf{V}\) depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), its components are:
\[
V_x = V_x(x, y), \quad V_y = V_y(x, y), \quad V_z = 0
\]
Substituting these into the curl expression:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\ V_x & V_y & 0 \end{vmatrix}
\]
Expanding the determinant:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial z} \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Since \(V_y\) and \(V_x\) are functions of \(x\) and \(y\) only, their partial derivatives with respect to \(z\) are zero. Thus:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

**Final Answer:**
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]

