To determine the spherical aberration of the mirror, we first calculate the focal length \( f \) using the radius of curvature \( R = 25 \) cm:  
\[
f = \frac{R}{2} = \frac{25}{2} = 12.5 \text{ cm}
\]  
The spherical aberration \( \delta \) is given by:  
\[
\delta = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{a^2}{R} - \frac{a^2}{f} \right)
\]  
where \( a \) is the radius of the mirror (half the diameter), \( R \) is the radius of curvature, and \( f \) is the focal length. Substituting the given values:  
\[
\delta = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{(10 \text{ cm})^2}{25 \text{ cm}} - \frac{(10 \text{ cm})^2}{12.5 \text{ cm}} \right) = \frac{1}{2} \left( 4 \text{ cm} - 8 \text{ cm} \right) = -2 \text{ cm}
\]  
The negative sign indicates that the spherical aberration is negative, which means the light rays are focused closer to the mirror than the theoretical focal point.  

Thus, the spherical aberration is \(\boxed{-2 \text{ cm}}\).

