The uncertainty principle states that the product of the uncertainty in a particle's energy (\(\Delta E\)) and the uncertainty in its lifetime (\(\Delta t\)) must be greater than or equal to Planck's constant divided by 2\(\pi\):  
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \frac{h}{2\pi}
\]  
For the \(\Sigma^+\) particle, the uncertainty in its lifetime is \(2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s. Using Planck's constant \(h = 6.626 \times 10^{-34}\) J·s, we can calculate the uncertainty in its rest energy:  
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{h}{2\pi \Delta t} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{2\pi \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23}} \approx 5.3 \times 10^{-12}\) J
\]  
Converting this energy to MeV:  
\[
\Delta E \approx 5.3 \times 10^{-12} \times \frac{1}{1.602 \times 10^{-13}} \approx 35.2 \text{ MeV}
\]  
Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the \(\Sigma^+\) rest energy would be:  
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} - 35.2 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1385 \text{ MeV} + 35.2 \text{ MeV}
\]  
or:  
\[
1350 \text{ MeV} \leq E \leq 1420 \text{ MeV}
\]  

Thus, the uncertainty in the measurement of the \(\Sigma^+\) rest energy is \(\boxed{35.2 \text{ MeV}}\).

