","The Coriolis effect plays a significant role in determining the direction of large-scale ocean currents such as the Gulf Stream and the Kidoushio current. It is a fictitious force that arises due to the Earth's rotation and affects the motion of any object moving over a distance. The Coriolis effect is not a real force, but a consequence of the constant motion of the Earth and the fixed nature of the reference frame (e.g., the Earth).  

The Coriolis effect is calculated as:  
\[
F_{\text{Coriolis}} = 2 \times \text{velocity} \times \text{angular velocity} \times \text{cos}(90 - \text{angle of motion})
\]  
where:  
- \( F_{\text{Coriolis}} \) is the Corillus force,  
- \(\text{velocity}\) is the velocity of the moving object,  
- \(\text{angular velocity}\) is the angular velocity of the Earth (approximately \( 2.5 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{rad/s} \)),  
- \(\text{angle of motion}\) is the angle between the object's velocity and the vertical axis.  

For example, in the Gulf Stream, the Coriolis effect causes the current to move toward the north in the south direction. The Coriolis effect is stronger at the top of the ocean (where the speed of the object is higher) and weaker at the bottom (where the speed of the object is lower).  

\boxed{
\text{The Coriolis effect causes the Gulf Stream to move toward the north in the south direction, and the Kidoushio current to move toward the north in the south direction.}
}
