"The vapour density of nitrogen peroxide (N₂O₄) is 25.67 at 100°C and 760 mm Hg. Calculate the percentage of NO₂ molecules in the gas at this temperature, given that N₂O₄ dissociates into NO₂ according to the reaction:  
\[ \text{N}_2\text{O}_4 \rightleftharpoons 2\text{NO}_2. \]","<bos><start_of_turn>user
The vapour density of nitrogen peroxide (N₂O₄) is 25.67 at 100°C and 760 mm Hg. Calculate the percentage of NO₂ molecules in the gas at this temperature, given that N₂O₄ dissociates into NO₂ according to the reaction:  
\[ \text{N}_2\text{O}_4 \rightleftharpoons 2\text{NO}_2. \]<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Calculate the molar mass of N₂O₄:**  
   - Nitrogen (N): 14.01 g/mol  
   - Oxygen (O): 16.00 g/mol  
   - Molar mass of N₂O₄ = (2 × 14.01) + (4 × 16.00) = 98.02 g/mol  

2. **Relate vapour density to molar mass:**  
   - Vapour density = (Molar mass of gas) / (Molar mass of hydrogen)  
   - Given vapour density = 25.67, and molar mass of hydrogen = 2 g/mol  
   - Molar mass of N₂O₄ = 25.67 × 2 = 51.34 g/mol  

3. **Calculate the degree of dissociation (\(\alpha\)):**  
   - The reaction is:  
     \[ \text{N}_2\text{O}_4 \rightleftharpoons 2\text{NO}_2. \]  
   - At equilibrium, the total molar mass of the gas is:  
     \[ \text{Molar mass at equilibrium} = \text{Molar mass of N}_2\text{O}_4 \times (1 - \alpha) + \text{Molar mass of NO}_2 \times 2\alpha. \]  
   - Substituting the molar masses:  
     \[ 51.34 = 98.02 \times (1 - \alpha) + 46 \times 2\alpha. \]  
   - Solving for \(\alpha\):  
     \[ 51.34 = 98.02 - 98.02\alpha + 92\alpha \]  
     \[ 56.68 = 16.02\alpha \]  
     \[ \alpha = 0.351 \]  

4. **Calculate the percentage of NO₂:**  
   - Percentage of NO₂ = \(\alpha \times 100\%\) = 0.351 × 100% = 35.1%  

**Final Answer:**  
The percentage of NO₂ molecules in the gas at 100°C and 760 mm Hg is \(\boxed{35.1\%}\).
