In summary, the addition of 4% copper and the formation of hard CuAl$_2$ particles in the aluminum alloy lead to a higher yield strength and a lower percent elongation compared to pure aluminum. This is due to the increased resistance to dislocation movement and the reduced ability of the material to deform and elongate before breaking.
