","(a) For the light to exit at normal incidence, the total deviation must be \( 90^\circ \). The prism is five-sided, so the angle of incidence at the first face must be \( 90^\circ - \beta \). The angle of refraction at the first face is \( \theta_1 = \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{\sin (90^\circ - \beta)}{n} \right) \). The angle of incidence at the second face is \( \theta_1 + \beta \), and the angle of refraction is \( \theta_2 = \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{\sin (\theta_1 + \beta)}{n} \right) \). The angle of incidence at the third face is \( \theta_2 + \beta \), and the angle of refraction is \( \theta_3 = \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{\sin (\theta_2 + \beta)}{n} \right) \). The angles of incidence and refraction for the fourth and fifth faces are the same in magnitude but opposite in sign. Thus, the total deviation is \( 2 \theta_1 + 2 \theta_2 + 2 \theta_3 + 2 \theta_4 + 2 \theta_5 = 90^\circ \). Solving for \( \beta \) is numerically challenging but possible.  

