","The entropy production rate \( \Pi_S \) is given by:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{Q}{T} - \frac{Q_0}{T}
\]
where \( Q \) is the heat added to the system and \( Q_0 \) is the heat removed from the system. In this case, the heat added to the system is the heat generated by the friction force, and the heat removed from the system is the heat lost to the surroundings.

The heat generated by the friction force is:
\[
Q = F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t
\]
where \( \Delta t \) is the time interval over which the heat is generated. The heat lost to the surroundings is:
\[
Q_0 = \frac{Q}{T}
\]
Substituting these into the entropy production rate:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t}{T} - \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t}{T^2} = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t}{T} \left(1 - \frac{1}{T}\right)
\]
Thus, the entropy production rate is:
\[
\boxed{\frac{F_{\text{fr}} v \Delta t}{T} \left(1 - \frac{1}{T}\right)}
\]
